 \section BLCissues Known Issues

 None.

 \section BLCIntro Introduction

 The boundary-layer conductance to heat describes the rate of energy transfer across the primitive boundary-layer for a given driving temperature difference. Currently, five different boundary-layer conductance models are available as part of this plug-in.

 The plug-in also gives the boundary-layer conductances to heat, water vapor and CO2 of each face of a primitive. The front face is the one the primitive normal points out of, and the back face is the other one. The faces of a leaf can exchange heat and mass at quite different rates: buoyancy makes the boundary layer on one face of a leaf warmer or cooler than the air stable and that on the other unstable, and in wind the windward face has a thinner boundary layer than the leeward face. The "Graefe" model (\ref BLC5) resolves these differences, and the "InclinedPlate" model (\ref BLC2) resolves the effect of buoyancy on the faces of a nearly horizontal plate; the other models split their conductance equally between the faces. The \ref EnergyBalanceModel, \ref PhotosynthesisModel and \ref StomatalConductanceModel plug-ins use the per-face conductances when they are present, so that the stomata on each face (set by primitive data "stomatal_sidedness", the fraction of the stomata on the front face) exchange gas through their own face's boundary layer. This matters most for leaves with their stomata on one face.



