 \section BLUse Using the Boundary Layer Conductance Model Plug-in

 \subsection BLPrimData Input Variables

 Inputs to the model are set by creating primitive variable data in the usual way. If a variable needed for a model input has not been create in the Context, the default value is assumed.

 \subsection BLCModels Boundary-layer Conductance Models

 There are five different built-in models for the boundary-layer conductance.  The boundary-layer conductance model is set using the \ref BLConductanceModel::setBoundaryLayerModel "setBoundaryLayerModel()" function, which takes as arguments the UUID(s) of primitives for which the model is to be set, and a string referencing the chosen model. Possible models are summarized in the table below and described in further detail below. If the \ref BLConductanceModel::setBoundaryLayerModel "setBoundaryLayerModel()" is called for some UUIDs but not others, the plug-in will assume the default model (Pohlhausen) for any primitives for which no model was explicitly set.

 It is also important to note that, by default, the length scale used to calculate the boundary-layer conductance is taken to be the square root of the surface area of the compound object the primitive belongs to (for example, the tile object forming a leaf), or of the primitive itself if it does not belong to an object. If this is not the relevant scale for boundary-layer development, set it with primitive data "object_length". This is usually necessary when using the boundary-layer conductance model for a sphere, whose length scale is its diameter, and for elongated leaves, whose relevant length is their dimension along the wind. The "Graefe" model does not use this length scale (see \ref BLC5).

 The five available models are described in detail below:

 <table>
 <tr><th>Model<th>string argument
 <tr><td>1. Pohlhausen Equation (default)<td>"Pohlhausen"
 <tr><td>2. Inclined Plate<td>"InclinedPlate"
 <tr><td>3. Laminar Sphere<td>"Sphere"
 <tr><td>4. Ground Surface<td>"Ground"
 <tr><td>5. Inclined Elliptical Leaf, Mixed Free-Forced Convection, Per Face<td>"Graefe"
 </table>

 An unknown model string is an error.

 \subsubsection BLC1 1. The Pohlhausen Equation (Laminar Flat Plate, Forced Convection)

 Pohlhausen (1921) solved the laminar thermal boundary layer on an isothermal flat plate parallel with the flow direction, infinitely wide in the spanwise direction and of length \f$L\f$ in the streamwise direction. The resulting average Nusselt number of one face of the plate is \f$Nu=0.664Re^{1/2}Pr^{1/3}\f$. Converted to a molar conductance with the properties of air near 20 \f$^\circ\f$C and standard pressure, this is the leaf boundary-layer conductance to heat given by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-1626-1">Campbell and Norman (1998)</a>, \f$0.135\sqrt{U/L}\f$ mol/m<sup>2</sup>-s for each face. The model sums it over the faces of the primitive:

 \f$g_H = 0.135 n_s\sqrt{\frac{U}{L}}\f$,

 where \f$U\f$ is the wind speed just outside of the primitive boundary-layer, \f$L\f$ is the characteristic length (see above), and \f$n_s\f$ is the number of primitive faces, which is determined by the value of primitive data "twosided_flag" (twosided\_flag=0 is single-sided and \f$n_s=1\f$, twosided\_flag=1 is two-sided and \f$n_s=2\f$). The two faces get equal conductances.

 The model has the following limitations:
 - The air properties are fixed, so the conductance does not change with air temperature or pressure.
 - \f$L\f$ should be the dimension of the object along the wind, but by default it is the square root of the object's area and does not depend on the wind direction.
 - The flow is laminar and forced only. Buoyancy is neglected, so the conductance is zero in still air and both faces are the same.
 - Pohlhausen's solution is for a smooth, isolated plate in a steady, non-turbulent stream. Leaves outdoors typically exchange heat faster than this, because of free-stream turbulence, leaf edges and leaf motion. The "Graefe" model (\ref BLC5) accounts for leaf edges, turbulence intensity and buoyancy.

 \subsubsection BLC2 2. Laminar Inclined Plate, Mixed Free-Forced Convection

 <a href="https://doi.org/10.1115/1.3247020">Chen et al. (1986)</a> provide correlations for laminar mixed free-forced convection in the boundary layer on an isothermal flat plate inclined at an angle \f$\gamma\f$ from the vertical (\f$\gamma=0^\circ\f$ for a vertical plate and \f$90^\circ\f$ for a horizontal one; \f$\gamma\f$ is also the angle of the primitive normal from the horizontal). The correlations give the heat transfer of the boundary layer on one face of the plate. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1115/1.3248035">Ramachandran et al. (1987)</a> measured the flows above and below an inclined plate and found them essentially the same, so the model applies the correlation to each face of the primitive and the summed conductance of a two-sided primitive is twice it. For nearly horizontal plates the faces differ, as described below. The model writes the conductance of each face (see \ref BLOutputData).

 The conductance of a face is \f$g_H=\rho_a\frac{\nu}{Pr\,L}Nu\f$, and for a plate inclined at \f$\gamma<75^\circ\f$ its average Nusselt number is

 \f$Nu=2F_1Re^{1/2}\left\{1\pm\left[\frac{2F_2\left(Gr\,\mathrm{cos}\,\gamma/Re^2\right)^{1/4}}{3F_1}\right]^3\right\}^{1/3},\f$

and for \f$\gamma\geq 75^\circ\f$

\f$Nu=2F_1Re^{1/2}\left\{1\pm\left[\frac{F_4\left(Gr/Re^{5/2}\right)^{1/5}Gr^{C(\gamma)}}{6\left[0.2+C(\gamma)\right]F_1}\right]^3\right\}^{1/3},\f$

where \f$\rho_a\f$, \f$\nu\f$, and \f$Pr\f$ are respectively the molar density, kinematic viscosity, and Prandtl number of air, \f$L\f$ is the characteristic length of the plate (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1115/1.3597463">Parkhurst 1968</a>), \f$Re\f$ is the Reynolds number based on \f$L\f$ and the local free-stream air velocity, and \f$Gr\f$ is the Grashof number which is defined here as

\f$Gr=\frac{g\beta\left|T_L-T_a\right|L^3}{\nu^2},\f$

where \f$g\f$ is the acceleration due to gravity, and \f$\beta\f$ is the volumetric thermal expansion coefficient which we approximate as the inverse of absolute ambient air temperature \f$1/T_a\f$ with \f$T_a\f$ in units of Kelvin. The constants in the correlations are defined as

\f$F_1=0.339Pr^{1/3}\left[1+\left(0.0468/Pr\right)^{2/3}\right]^{-1/4},\f$

\f$F_2=0.75Pr^{1/2}\left[2.5\left(1+2Pr^{1/2}+2Pr\right)\right]^{-1/4},\f$

\f$F_4=Pr^{1/2}\left[0.25+1.6Pr^{1/2}\right]^{-1}\left(Pr/5\right)^{0.2+C(\gamma)},\f$

\f$C(\gamma)=0.070\left(\mathrm{cos}\,\gamma\right)^{1/2}.\f$

Chen et al. (1986) print the coefficient of \f$F_1\f$ as 0.399, but the correlation of Churchill and Ozoe (1973, J. Heat Transfer 95:416) that they cite has 0.339, which reproduces the classical laminar flat-plate value (0.332 in Ramachandran et al. 1987); 0.339 is used here.

The plus and minus signs correspond to buoyancy assisting and opposing the forced flow, respectively. For \f$\gamma<75^\circ\f$ buoyancy drives the flow up the slope along a heated plate and down it along a cooled one, on both faces. If primitive data "wind_direction" is given, the buoyancy assists when the wind has a component up the slope of a heated plate (or down the slope of a cooled one) and opposes otherwise; if the wind direction is unknown or across the slope, it is taken to assist. For \f$\gamma\geq 75^\circ\f$ the buoyancy acts across the plate, and it assists the flow on the face it rises from (the upper face of a heated plate, the lower face of a cooled one) and opposes it on the other face. With opposing buoyancy the correlation holds only while the term in square brackets, the ratio of the free to the forced convection Nusselt number, is less than 1; beyond that the boundary layer separates and free convection dominates. The model then uses the larger of the opposing mixed-convection value and the pure free-convection value of that face, which is the term in square brackets times \f$2F_1Re^{1/2}\f$ (half of it for the lower face of a heated, nearly horizontal plate).

When the wind speed is zero (\f$Re=0\f$) the mixed-convection correlations above cannot be evaluated, so the model instead uses pure free convection with the laminar natural-convection correlations of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/cite.330260716">McAdams (1954)</a>: \f$Nu=0.54\left(Gr\,\mathrm{sin}\,\gamma\right)^{1/4}\f$ for the face of a nearly horizontal plate (\f$\gamma\geq 75^\circ\f$) that buoyancy rises from and \f$Nu=0.27\left(Gr\,\mathrm{sin}\,\gamma\right)^{1/4}\f$ for the other face, and \f$Nu=0.59\left(Gr\,\mathrm{cos}\,\gamma\right)^{1/4}\f$ for both faces of a plate inclined at \f$\gamma<75^\circ\f$. These free-convection correlations are not the limit of the mixed-convection correlations as the wind speed tends to zero, so the conductance jumps when the wind speed reaches zero: for a 10 cm plate 5 K warmer than 300 K air, the summed zero-wind conductance is about 1.8 times the low-wind value for a horizontal plate and about 1.3 times for a vertical plate.

Chen et al. (1986) mention that the equation for \f$\gamma< 75^\circ\f$ is valid for \f$10^3\leq Re \leq 10^5\f$, and the equation for \f$\gamma\geq 75^\circ\f$ is valid for \f$10^3\leq GrPr \leq 10^9\f$.  We expect leaf Reynolds numbers somewhere between \f$5\times 10^3\f$ and \f$5\times 10^4\f$. Average \f$Gr Pr\f$ values are usually on the order of \f$10^6\f$. When \f$T_L \approx T_a\f$, it is possible for \f$Gr Pr\f$ to drop below \f$10^3\f$, however in these cases the net radiation is usually nearly zero and convective heat fluxes are low anyway.

 \subsubsection BLC3 3. Laminar flow around a sphere

 <a href="https://books.google.com/books?id=L5FnNlIaGfcC&dq=bird+lightfoot+Transport+Phenomena&lr=&source=gbs_navlinks_s">Bird et al. (1960)</a> provides correlation for forced convection heat transfer in laminar flow around a sphere

 \f$g_H = \frac{0.00164}{D} + 0.110\sqrt{\frac{U}{D}}\f$,

 where \f$D\f$ is the sphere diameter, and \f$U\f$ is the wind speed outside of the sphere boundary-layer.

 \subsubsection BLC4 4. Flow over bare ground

 <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0168-1923(99)00005-2">Kustas and Norman (1999)</a> suggest a simple relationship for the convective heat conductance over flat, bare ground:

 \f$g_H = 0.166+0.5U,\f$

 where \f$U\f$ is the wind speed at a height above the soil surface where the effect of the soil surface roughness is minimal; typically 0.05 to 0.2 m.

 \subsubsection BLC5 5. Inclined elliptical leaf, mixed free-forced convection, per face

 This model is a C++ port of the general model of the <a href="https://github.com/Code4Plants/Leaf-Boundary-Layer">leaf-boundary-layer</a> package by Jan Graefe (Leibniz Institute of Vegetable and Ornamental Crops, v1.1.0, LGPL-2.1-or-later). Its free-convection core is the model of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agrformet.2022.108884">Graefe, Grosch and Bitterlich (2022)</a> for inclined elliptical leaves, and the forced and mixed convection, laminar-turbulent transition, angle-of-attack and scalar-conductance extensions are those of the package. As its author requests, please cite both the paper and the package (Graefe, J., 2026, leaf-conductance: boundary-layer conductance of leaves under free, forced and mixed convection, version 1.1.0) when using this model. It gives the conductances of the upper and lower faces of a flat elliptical leaf of length \f$L\f$ and width \f$W\f$ (\f$L\geq W\f$) separately, for heat, water vapor and CO2. The upper face is the one facing up, which is the front face if the primitive normal points up.

 <b>Free convection.</b> The flux-proportional Nusselt numbers (m<sup>-1</sup>) of the upper (U) and lower (L) face of a leaf warmer than the air are (Eq. 3 of Graefe et al. 2022)

 \f$Nu_{i,U}=p_3L_i^{-1/4}\sin^{p_4}\theta\,Ra^{1/4},\quad Nu_{i,L}=p_3L_i^{-1/4}\sin^{1/4}\theta\,Ra^{1/4},\quad Nu_{h,U}=p_5L_h^{-1/4}\cos^{1/4}\theta\,Ra^{1/4},\quad Nu_{h,L}=p_6L_h^{-0.4}\cos^{0.2}\theta\,Ra^{0.2},\f$

 \f$Nu_c=5.3\sin(0.0338\,L/W+0.607)/\sqrt{S},\qquad Nu_F=\left\{\left[Nu_i^{p_1}+Nu_h^{p_1}\right]^{p_2/p_1}+Nu_c^{p_2}\right\}^{1/p_2},\f$

 where \f$\theta\f$ is the angle of the leaf normal from vertical, \f$S=\pi LW/4\f$ is the leaf area, \f$L_h=S/P\f$ is the characteristic length of a horizontal leaf with the perimeter \f$P\f$ implied by the dissection index of the ellipse, \f$L_i\f$ is the characteristic length of an inclined leaf, from the mean chord length along the direction of steepest ascent, \f$Ra=g|\Delta T|Pr/(T_a\nu^2)\f$ is a Rayleigh number without length scale, and \f$p_1\ldots p_6\f$ = 6, 1.42, 0.513, 0.4, 0.202, 0.492. For a leaf cooler than the air the upper and lower faces exchange expressions. Note that for a nearly horizontal leaf the fitted parameters give the stably stratified face (the lower face of a warm leaf) a larger conductance than the unstable face, unlike the classical correlations for large heated plates. The conduction limit \f$Nu_c\f$ makes small leaves exchange heat even in still air. On the unstable face, a turbulent free-convection contribution \f$0.14Ra^{1/3}\f$ is blended in as \f$(Nu_F^6+Nu_{turb}^6)^{1/6}\f$, which matters only for leaves of decimeter size and large temperature differences.

 <b>Forced convection.</b> The forced convection of each face is the flat-plate average Nusselt number of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1115/1.4046795">Lienhard (2020)</a>, laminar \f$0.664Re^{1/2}Pr^{1/3}\f$ up to a transition onset \f$Re_l=3.63\times 10^5\,TI^{-1.25}\f$ (bounded to \f$[2\times10^3, 5\times10^5]\f$, with the turbulence intensity \f$TI\f$ in %), then transitional and turbulent. The Reynolds number is based on the streamline length: the horizontal wind is projected onto the leaf plane, and the length of the leaf along that direction is weighted as in Eq. 4 of Graefe et al. (2022). If the wind direction is not given, or the wind is normal to the leaf, the orientation-averaged chord length is used. A finite-plate enhancement (1.161) and factors for the windward face, \f$1+0.226\sin\beta\f$, and the leeward face, \f$w(1+0.574\sin 2\beta)+0.833(1-w)\f$ with \f$w=\exp[-(\beta/31.2^\circ)^2]\f$, account for the angle of attack \f$\beta\f$ of the wind on the leaf; these were fitted by the package author to 815 thin-plate and leaf measurements.

 <b>Mixed convection and scalars.</b> Free and forced convection are combined on each face as \f$Nu=(Nu_{F,free}^3+Nu_{F,forced}^3)^{1/3}\f$, and the conductance to heat is \f$\kappa Nu\f$ with the thermal diffusivity \f$\kappa\f$ of air at the film temperature, converted to mol/m<sup>2</sup>-s with the molar density of air. The conductances to water vapor and CO2 are \f$g_s=g_H(D_s/\kappa)^k\f$, where \f$D_s\f$ is the molecular diffusivity and the exponent \f$k\f$ is weighted between the transport regimes (1 for conduction, 0.75 for laminar and 2/3 for turbulent free convection, 2/3 for laminar and 0.4 for turbulent forced convection).

 <b>Leaf shape.</b> The length, width and long axis of the leaf are those of an equivalent ellipse of the primitive's parent object, which is taken to be the whole leaf: the ellipse has the aspect ratio of the object's principal second moments of area and the object's area. For a primitive without a parent object the primitive itself is used. The primitive data "leaf_length" and "leaf_width" override the size. The inclination and the angle of attack are those of the primitive's own normal, and the long axis is projected onto the primitive's plane. A primitive whose plane contains no part of the leaf's long axis, as can occur for strongly folded or rolled leaves, is an error, as are voxels.

 \subsection BLCSet Setting the Boundary-layer Conductance Model To Be Used

 ~~~~~~{.cpp}
  // Declare the Context and add two primitives
  Context context;

  uint UUID0 = context.addPatch();

  uint UUID1 = context.addPatch();

  // Initialize the boundary-layer conductance Model
  BLConductanceModel boundarylayerconductance(&context);

  // This changes the boundary-layer conductance model for all (both) primitives
  boundarylayerconductance.setBoundaryLayerModel( "InclinedPlate" );

  // This changes the boundary-layer conductance model for the second primitive (while the first will keep the model set above)
  boundarylayerconductance.setBoundaryLayerModel( UUID1, "Ground" );
 ~~~~~~

 \subsection BLCRun Running the Model

 The model can be run to calculate the boundary-layer conductance for all primitives or a sub-set of primitives using the appropriate run function below.

 <table>
 <caption>Functions to perform boundary-layer conductance model calculations.</caption>
 <tr><th>Model Run Function <th>Description
 <tr><td>\ref BLConductanceModel::run "BLConductanceModel::run()" <td>Run model calculations for all primitives in the Context.
 <tr><td>\ref BLConductanceModel::run "BLConductanceModel::run()" <td>Run model calculations for a select set of primitives in the Context, which are specified by a vector of their UUIDs.
 </table>

*/
